00:12:17.000 --> 00:13:00.000
Because he wanted his face on a coin. But the ability to put almost anything you wanted on a coin was not enough on its own to pave the way for the state quarters program. For that to happen, U.S. coins would need to undergo one more critical transformation, this time to their metal content. Remember, up until this point, silver coins were still made of silver. They had that inherent metal value. But in the 1960s, that ended. President Lyndon Johnson got rid of all of our pretty, shiny silver coins and replaced them with something called clad coins. In clad coinage, a piece of low-cost, kind of ugly metal, is clad in a thin layer of something a little bit prettier.
因为他想把自己的脸印在硬币上。但仅仅能在硬币上放任何你想要的东西，并不足以铺平州纪念币计划的道路。要实现这一点，美国硬币还需要经历另一次关键的转变，这次是关于它们的金属含量。记住，直到这一点，银币仍然是用白银制成的。它们具有固有的金属价值。但在20世纪60年代，这种情况结束了。林登·约翰逊总统取消了我们所有漂亮、闪亮的银币，取而代之的是一种叫做“包层硬币”的东西。在包层硬币中，一块低成本、有点丑的金属被包裹在一层稍微漂亮一点的薄层中。

24
00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:40.000
It's a much cheaper way to make a coin. So in 1965, looking for ways to save money, Johnson convinced Congress to stop putting silver in coins. Instead, he had them minted from copper and then covered in nickel. They were nicknamed Johnson sandwiches. Jesse Kraft says that prior to this, if a national government ever debased its currency, they would only dare to do it very gradually, changing the coins metal content a little at a time over the course of decades. LBJ, God bless them, just went ahead and did it all at once. In 1964, literally all the coins are silver, 1965, none of them are silver.
这是一种制造硬币便宜得多的方法。因此，在1965年，为了寻找省钱的方法，约翰逊说服国会停止在硬币中加入白银。相反，他让他们用铜铸造，然后覆盖上镍。它们被称为“约翰逊三明治”。杰西·克拉夫特说，在此之前，如果一个国家政府要贬低其货币，他们只会非常缓慢地进行，在几十年内一点一点地改变硬币的金属含量。林登·约翰逊，上帝保佑他们，直接一次性完成了。1964年，所有的硬币都是银的；1965年，它们都不是银的。

25
00:13:40.000 --> 00:14:15.000
So it kind of like created valueless coins. So it's actually one of the most significant things that's ever happened since perhaps the beginning of coins themselves. And no one seemed to care. And it's true. No one really cared. Everyone just carried on as if we were all still lugging these little pieces of silver around and continued spending and accepting U.S. coins like nothing had changed. But for the mint, everything had changed. because this strange metallurgical bait and switch meant that suddenly a coin like a quarter costs way less than a quarter to make.
所以它创造了毫无价值的硬币。这实际上是自硬币诞生以来发生的最重要的事情之一。而且似乎没人关心。确实如此。没人真正在意。每个人都像我们还在拖着这些小银片一样继续生活，并继续像什么都没发生一样消费和接受美国硬币。但对于铸币局来说，一切都变了。因为这种奇怪的冶金诱饵和转换意味着像25美分这样的硬币制造成本突然远低于25美分。

26
00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:49.000
And that discrepancy between the production cost of the coin and its face value allowed the meant to engage in a money-making scheme beloved by governments the world over. It's called seniorage. Okay, seniorage. It's really a simple concept. This is Philip Deal. These days, he's the president of U.S. Money Reserve, a coin's. seller. A few decades ago, though, he was director of the U.S. Mint. And he says, to understand seniorage, you have to think of the Mint as a business, a business whose product is coins.
硬币的生产成本与其面值之间的差异使铸币局能够参与一种深受世界各国政府喜爱的赚钱计划。这被称为“铸币税”（seniorage）。好的，铸币税。这真的是一个简单的概念。这是菲利普·迪尔。如今，他是美国货币储备公司的总裁，一家硬币销售商。不过，几十年前，他是美国铸币局局长。他说，要理解铸币税，你必须把铸币局看作一家企业，一家产品是硬币的企业。

27
00:14:49.000 --> 00:15:30.000
And that is, like any product in business, you hope to produce it at a cost that is less than what you sell it for. So the goal for the mint is to make coins as cheaply as possible, but to still sell them at their face value so that they can turn a profit, just like any other product. And when you do that with coins, that's called seniorage. So seniorage is the profit the mint makes from selling a coin for more than a cost to produce it. Like Philip said, simple. Now, if you're wondering who would be stupid enough to pay 25 cents for a coin that only costs a few cents to make, well, you are. And me too.
就像商业中的任何产品一样，你希望以低于售价的成本生产它。因此，铸币局的目标是尽可能便宜地制造硬币，但仍以其面值出售，以便他们可以获利，就像任何其他产品一样。当你用硬币这样做时，这就叫铸币税。所以铸币税是铸币局通过以高于生产成本的价格出售硬币而获得的利润。就像菲利普说的，很简单。现在，如果你想知道谁会傻到花25美分去买一枚制造成本只有几美分的硬币，嗯，就是你。还有我。

28
00:15:30.000 --> 00:16:03.000
And me. We all end up paying full face value for these coins, thanks to a chain of sale that starts with the Mint's largest customer. the independent Federal Reserve Bank. Which, just for clarity, operates independently of the federal government. The Federal Reserve, they pay us, the face value of the coin, and then those coins are distributed to the regional Federal Reserve banks, and the regional Federal Reserve banks then take orders from the banking system in the United States, and the coins are delivered to the banks.
还有我。我们最终都为这些硬币支付了全额面值，这要归功于从铸币局最大的客户——独立的联邦储备银行开始的销售链。顺便说明一下，它独立于联邦政府运作。美联储向我们支付硬币的面值，然后这些硬币被分发到地区联邦储备银行，地区联邦储备银行随后接受美国银行系统的订单，硬币被运送到银行。

29
00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:42.000
Let me translate, at the risk of making this part, a little too simple. The mint makes coins, again, let's say quarters, as cheaply as it can. The banks then buy those quarters at face value from the mint. Businesses buy quarters at face value from banks. And when we get change from the cash registers at those businesses, we get those quarters. So everyone in that sequence, banks, businesses, people, pay 25 cents for something that the mint originally made for much, much less. Once a quarter gets old and worn out, the U.S. government can end up buying it, back and recycling the metal. But that can take a long time. And that coin might have circulated
让我翻译一下，冒着把这部分说得太简单的风险。铸币局尽可能便宜地制造硬币，比如25美分。银行然后以面值从铸币局购买这些25美分。企业以面值从银行购买25美分。当我们从这些企业的收银台找零时，我们得到了这些25美分。所以这个序列中的每个人——银行、企业、个人——都为铸币局最初以低得多的成本制造的东西支付了25美分。一旦25美分硬币变旧磨损，美国政府最终可以买回并回收金属。但这可能需要很长时间。那枚硬币可能已经流通了……

30
00:16:42.000 --> 00:17:35.000
for 30 years. And so for us, the mint, that coin is basically like a 30-year long-term loan, interest-free. So in the end, the only one turning a profit is the U.S. government. But the truth is, this approach only worked well for a little while. When the U.S. first started making significant seniorage off of coins in the 1960s, it was a good bet. The difference between the production cost and the sales price ensured that the mint made about four and a half cents off of every nickel, nine and a half cents off of every dime, and 24 cents off of every quarter. Even the penny made a little bit of money. But as the price of metals like copper rose, so did the cost of making coins. As early as 1974, the cost of making the penny was already approaching its face value.
30年。所以对我们铸币局来说，那枚硬币基本上就像一笔30年的长期无息贷款。所以最终，唯一获利的是美国政府。但事实是，这种方法只在短时间内有效。当美国在20世纪60年代首次从硬币中获得大量铸币税时，这是一个不错的赌注。生产成本和销售价格之间的差异确保了铸币局每枚镍币赚取约4.5美分，每枚角币赚取9.5美分，每枚25美分硬币赚取24美分。即使是便士也赚了一点钱。但随着铜等金属价格的上涨，制造硬币的成本也随之上升。早在1974年，制造便士的成本就已经接近其面值。

31
00:17:35.000 --> 00:18:17.000
So the mint futzed with the metal ratios again, just trying to make this thing for even cheaper. They up to the zinc and lowered the copper, which again worked at first, but not for long. So when Philip Deal became the director of the U.S. Mint in 1994, one of his main goals was to stop and ideally reverse this decline. And so when I was directing, the MET, 30 years ago, I was the first MENT director to recommend the elimination of the penny, and it was long overdue then. Unfortunately, Philip did not have the necessary sway to kill the penny. America would have to wait for someone else to make that one happen.
所以铸币局再次调整了金属比例，试图以更便宜的价格制造这种东西。他们增加了锌，减少了铜，这起初确实有效，但没持续多久。因此，当菲利普·迪尔在1994年成为美国铸币局局长时，他的主要目标之一是停止并理想地扭转这种下降趋势。所以当我在30年前指导铸币局时，我是第一位建议取消便士的局长，当时已经早该取消了。不幸的是，菲利普没有必要的权力来废除便士。美国将不得不等待其他人来实现这一点。

32
00:18:17.000 --> 00:18:57.000
But there was another more exciting idea to boost senior edge that was being bounced around the mint at the same time. A scheme to increase the sales of one of their biggest earners. the quarter. And after a couple years, I started thinking about whether or not it was possible, and I became convinced we could do it. At that time, in the mid-90s, every quarter, thanks to its high face value, was still netting the mint about 22 cents in senior age. And every year, some of those quarters would fall out of circulation. People lost them, accidentally threw them out, or put them in coin jars in the car, which meant every year the banks had to ask the mint for more quarters
但当时铸币局还在酝酿另一个更令人兴奋的增加铸币税的想法。一个增加他们最大收入来源之一——25美分硬币销售额的计划。几年后，我开始思考这是否可能，我确信我们可以做到。那时，在90年代中期，每一枚25美分硬币，由于其高面值，仍然为铸币局带来约22美分的铸币税。每年，其中一些25美分硬币会退出流通。人们丢失了它们，不小心扔掉了它们，或者把它们放在车里的硬币罐里，这意味着每年银行都必须向铸币局要求更多的25美分硬币……

33
00:18:57.000 --> 00:19:44.000
to replace all those missing coins, make up the gap, and ensure that the country didn't run out of change. That, of course, was good for the U.S. government, because selling more quarters to the banks generated more seniorage for the mint. But what if there was a way to get people to take even more quarters out of circulation every year? Well, one way was to convince people to start collecting them. Only, the mint couldn't just rely on hardcore collectors, like the ones I met at the convention to get the job done. The idea was to take billions of dollars worth of quarters out of circulation. If it was ever going to do that, the mint would somehow have to convince ordinary people, people who rarely even thought about quarters, to start collecting them too. And what better way
来替换所有那些丢失的硬币，弥补缺口，并确保国家不会用完零钱。这当然对美国政府有利，因为向银行出售更多的25美分硬币为铸币局产生了更多的铸币税。但如果有一种方法能让人们每年从流通中取出更多的25美分硬币呢？嗯，一种方法是说服人们开始收藏它们。只是，铸币局不能仅仅依靠我在大会上遇到的那些硬核收藏家来完成这项工作。这个想法是从流通中取出价值数十亿美元的25美分硬币。如果它真的要做到这一点，铸币局必须以某种方式说服普通人，那些很少想到25美分硬币的人，也开始收藏它们。还有什么比这更好的方法呢……

34
00:19:44.000 --> 00:20:36.000
to encourage ordinary Americans who start collecting quarters than by issuing a bunch of special designs, say 50 of them. And so I'm sort of famous there for having created the 50 state quarters program. Sneaky, perhaps. Elaborate? Sure. Profitable. Definitely. The idea had been circling among coin fans for years. Canada's mint had already done something similar, and it made money. But Philip couldn't just order the mint to make a bunch of new designs. After all, the Mint, by this point, had been stamping quarters with the same design of George Washington in profile since 1932. Making lots of new designs required lots more resources. To make the state quarters program happen, the Mint needed an advocate in Washington.
鼓励普通美国人开始收藏25美分硬币，那就是发行一堆特殊设计，比如50种。所以，我因为创造了50州纪念币计划而闻名。狡猾吗？也许。精心策划？当然。有利可图？绝对。这个想法在硬币爱好者中已经流传多年。加拿大铸币局已经做过类似的事情，而且赚了钱。但菲利普不能仅仅命令铸币局制造一堆新设计。毕竟，到那时为止，铸币局自1932年以来一直用同样的乔治·华盛顿侧面像来压印25美分硬币。制造大量新设计需要更多的资源。为了实现州纪念币计划，铸币局需要在华盛顿有一个倡导者。

35
00:20:36.000 --> 00:21:07.000
So Philip went to a congressman from Delaware named Mike Castle and laid out his idea. I said, now, this has tremendous education potential. Each one of the 50 states will have designs emblematic of the state. People in the state and the end, students will become excited about it. Philip told him there was no way they could do all these coins in one year. He was thinking they'd roll them out five a year for a decade. But he didn't just want to release them alphabetically. That's boring. We're not teaching anybody anything.
所以菲利普去找了一位来自特拉华州的国会议员迈克·卡斯尔，并提出了他的想法。我说，现在，这具有巨大的教育潜力。50个州中的每一个都将拥有象征该州的设计。州内的人们，最终，学生们会对此感到兴奋。菲利普告诉他，他们不可能在一年内完成所有这些硬币。他想的是十年内每年推出五枚。但他不想只是按字母顺序发布它们。那太无聊了。我们什么也没教给任何人。

36
00:21:07.000 --> 00:21:36.000
Instead, Philip suggested to the congressman from Delaware that they released the coins in the order that the states ratified the Constitution. And it just so happened that Delaware is the first state. And I knew that I could sell him on that with that line. And man, he bought. And he became such a champion of that. And I needed a champion. The Mint needed the Treasury's approval,
相反，菲利普向特拉华州的国会议员建议，他们按照各州批准宪法的顺序发布硬币。碰巧特拉华州是第一个州。我知道我可以用那句话说服他。天哪，他买账了。他成为了那个计划的拥护者。我需要一个拥护者。铸币局需要财政部的批准，

37
00:21:36.000 --> 00:22:02.000
and the Treasury at the time did not like the idea. Too many things could go wrong. They wanted a study done. Everybody in Washington knows that studies are designed to kill proposals. It's just always. So the Treasury ran some numbers. Philip and his colleagues did too, how much seniorage each quarter would net the government, and also how many additional quarters they would get to sell to the banks
而当时的财政部不喜欢这个主意。太多的事情可能会出错。他们想要进行一项研究。华盛顿的每个人都知道，研究旨在扼杀提案。总是这样。所以财政部运行了一些数字。菲利普和他的同事们也做了，每枚25美分硬币将为政府带来多少铸币税，以及他们将额外向银行出售多少25美分硬币……

38
00:22:02.000 --> 00:22:32.000
due to people collecting them and taking them out of circulation. We looked at what's going to be the incremental increase in demand for quarters from people who want to pull them out of circulation and collect them. And the models came up with a range of 2.6 billion to 3.5 billion. In other words, the amount of quarters people took out and kept in their private collection would generate the government at least $2.6 billion in profit.
因为人们收藏它们并将它们从流通中取出。我们研究了那些想要将它们从流通中取出并收藏的人对25美分硬币的需求将会有多大的增量增长。模型得出的范围是26亿到35亿。换句话说，人们取出并保留在私人收藏中的25美分硬币数量将为政府带来至少26亿美元的利润。

39
00:22:32.000 --> 00:23:04.000
And the Treasury ended up recommending the program. And man, as the deputy secretary, I was at that meeting, Deputy Secretary of the Treasury was hot when his hand-picked committee made that recommendation. Congress gave the meant the go-ahead in late 1997, and just over a year later in 1999, the state quarters went live. Hi-ho, Kermit the Frog here, announcing the new 50 state quarters from the United States men. We employed Kermit the Frog.
财政部最终推荐了这个项目。天哪，作为副部长，我在那次会议上，当他亲自挑选的委员会做出那个建议时，财政部副部长很生气。国会在1997年底给了铸币局绿灯，一年多后的1999年，州纪念币上线了。嗨，我是科米蛙，宣布美国铸币局发行新的50州纪念币。我们雇用了科米蛙。

40
00:23:04.000 --> 00:23:28.000
I wanted Kermit because he was the perfect spokes frog for our product, because the appeal was so broad. Each quarter tells something about the state it represents. This one's New Jersey. It was the first time in decades that there was any significant change made to a U.S. coin. And it was exciting. Incredible bus.
我想要科米蛙，因为它是我们产品的完美代言蛙，因为吸引力非常广泛。每枚25美分硬币都讲述了它所代表的州的一些情况。这是新泽西州的。这是几十年来美国硬币首次发生任何重大变化。这令人兴奋。令人难以置信的轰动。

41
00:23:28.000 --> 00:23:48.000
50 state quarters from the United States men. The most exciting change America's ever seen. Philip and his team had done their research. They knew people loved the treasure hunt aspect of collecting. So they worked the... that into the program. All U.S. coins have mint marks, little letters on the front, indicating at which meant the coin was made.
美国铸币局发行的50州纪念币。美国见过的最令人兴奋的变化。菲利普和他的团队做了研究。他们知道人们喜欢收藏的寻宝方面。所以他们把这一点融入了计划中。所有美国硬币都有铸币标记，正面有小字母，表明硬币是在哪个铸币厂制造的。

42
00:23:48.000 --> 00:24:27.000
So they made sure every state quarter bore one of two mint marks, P for the Philadelphia mint, or D for Denver, so people would have twice as many coins to collect. The states especially loved it. Governors got their constituents involved, submitting design concepts and voting on their favorites. And that was a brilliant. marketing move because you got all this free, earn publicity as each state's coin was coming up for production and people would anxiously await the coins release.
所以他们确保每枚州纪念币都带有两个铸币标记之一，费城铸币厂的P或丹佛的D，这样人们就有两倍的硬币可以收集。各州特别喜欢它。州长们让他们的选民参与进来，提交设计概念并投票选出他们最喜欢的。这是一个绝妙的营销举措，因为随着每个州的硬币即将投入生产，你获得了所有这些免费的、赚来的宣传，人们会焦急地等待硬币的发布。

43
00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:43.000
That is true. If you were around when the state quarters first debuted, like I was, you will remember this. They were like the weather. Everyone talked about them all the time. Everyone. Kids loved it. Their parents loved it. We figured teenagers weren't cared a bit.
这是真的。如果你像我一样在州纪念币刚亮相时就在那里，你会记得这一点。它们就像天气一样。每个人都在谈论它们。每个人。孩子们喜欢它。他们的父母喜欢它。我们以为青少年根本不在乎。

44
00:24:43.000 --> 00:25:10.000
I mean, you know, we were competing with computer games. You know, how can coins compete with that? Hugely successful with teenagers, too. In fact, I remember standing in lines of music concerts or restaurants and being around teenagers and young 20-year-olds who'd be talking about the corners. And at times it seemed like almost everyone collected them, or at least would hold on to a few of their favorites.
我的意思是，你知道，我们在和电脑游戏竞争。你知道，硬币怎么能和那个竞争呢？在青少年中也非常成功。事实上，我记得站在音乐会或餐馆的队伍里，周围是谈论硬币的青少年和20岁出头的年轻人。有时似乎几乎每个人都在收集它们，或者至少会保留一些他们最喜欢的。

45
00:25:10.000 --> 00:25:29.000
To this day, loath to part with a Connecticut quarter. I mean, have you seen this thing? It is gorgeous. Soon, the private market was cashing in on it, too, selling all sorts of accessories. Are you like me and my family collecting the new U.S. quarters? Problem is, where do you put them? It's the USA Coin Collection album.
